1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,252
This crypto coin took in over four billion dollars. No true public blockchain, prosecutors said.

2
00:00:06,152 --> 00:00:08,876
So what were three and a half million people actually buying?

3
00:00:09,126 --> 00:00:12,946
OneCoin. Sold through a pyramid-style sales network. Sound familiar?

4
00:00:13,196 --> 00:00:17,292
Real coins have a public ledger anyone can check. Hit like if you never have.

5
00:00:17,592 --> 00:00:21,201
OneCoin said its blockchain was private. So— trust them, basically.

6
00:00:22,101 --> 00:00:24,817
And the price? The company just set it, prosecutors said.

7
00:00:25,717 --> 00:00:28,208
Send this to the friend who's still pitching you coins.

8
00:00:28,508 --> 00:00:32,907
It gets worse. Prosecutors said they handed out coins that weren't even in their own ledger.

9
00:00:33,207 --> 00:00:37,097
And the co-founder? Karl Sebastian Greenwood pleaded guilty. Twenty years.

10
00:00:37,347 --> 00:00:41,311
So what'd they call those coins? Follow, 'cause this page reads the fine print.

11
00:00:42,068 --> 00:00:44,942
Fake coins. Wait, what? Their own term, prosecutors said.

12
00:00:45,842 --> 00:00:48,768
Ever checked a coin's blockchain yourself? Yes or no.
